{
  "_notes": "Hand-authored park map (M2.6: tile-set parcels). World units = tiles (1 tile = 1.0); x grows east, y grows south; the visitor gate is on the south fence. The park interior is a park.w x park.h tile grid and EVERY interior tile is exactly one of: a walkway tile (its centre lies on a walkway centre-line segment), a plaza tile (inside the plaza block), a facility tile, or one tile of exactly one parcel. Zero blank tiles (checked by src/tiles.js validateParcels at load and by tools/validate-parcels.mjs). Parcels are connected polyominoes {id, tiles:[[x,y],...]} with NO biome of their own (M4): unowned land is neutral scrub and the player picks Desert / Plains / Marsh in the buy dialog, so price = chosen biome plot_cost x area_factor(tiles) and the choice lives in the save (state.parcels[id].biome), fence cost/upkeep = per-segment x outline edges (tile edges not shared with another tile of the same parcel), capacity = tiles x space_per_tile. 'shape' is a display hint only. Walkways are centre-line segments through tile centres (x.5, y.5) with a paving width w; every parcel has at least one outline edge facing a walkway or plaza tile (that is where visitors stand to look in). Projection pixel constants live under 'projection' so render/projection.js has no layout numbers of its own. M3: the forecourt strip (park.strip_h) is deep enough for the original-style big lot: a parking lot up to 7.3 tiles wide and 6 car rows deep, with the tram stop, visitor center, office and vet clinic in a cluster on the right. Strip facilities are authored as explicit rects (x0,y0,x1,y1) because they sit outside the tile grid; a tier's `render` in facilities.json scales inside that rect.",
